**Atrial fibrillation (AF)** is a heart condition that causes an **irregular and often fast heartbeat**. The top chambers of the heart (the atria) beat chaotically, so the heart does not pump as well. Around **1.5 million people** in the UK have AF, and many more may have it without knowing.

## Symptoms

- a **fluttering, thumping or racing** heartbeat (palpitations)
- an **irregular pulse**
- feeling **breathless**, tired or dizzy
- chest discomfort

Some people have **no symptoms**. AF can come and go (**paroxysmal**) or be there all the time (**persistent** or **permanent**).

## Why AF matters

Because blood does not flow smoothly, clots can form in the heart. If a clot travels to the brain it can cause a **stroke**. AF also makes **heart failure** more likely.

## What raises the risk?

- getting older
- high blood pressure, heart disease, heart valve problems or heart failure
- diabetes, obesity, sleep apnoea and an overactive thyroid
- drinking a lot of alcohol

## Getting diagnosed

AF is diagnosed with an **ECG**, a test that records your heart's rhythm. If it comes and goes, you may wear a small monitor for a day or more. Some smart watches can pick up signs of AF, but you still need an ECG to confirm it.

Read about [treatment for atrial fibrillation](/conditions/heart-and-circulation/atrial-fibrillation/treatment/).

{% callout type="danger" title="Call 999" %}
Call **999** if you have chest pain, severe breathlessness or fainting, or signs of a stroke: **F**ace drooping, **A**rm weakness, **S**peech problems, **T**ime to call 999.
{% /callout %}
